   "ResearcherNotesProject": "GC Aug 2026: Environmental impact assessment published in Nov 2025, submitted for public review in Jan 2026. No clear indication that construction has begun yet. Island Power Producers' CEO said the timeline to completion is relatively short once construction begins. \"From construction to operation, we’re looking at about 12 to 13 months.\"\nhttps://ewnews.com/island-power-outlines-timeline-for-arawak-cay-lng-development/\nThe LNG terminal will have 15,000 m3 of LNG of storage capacity, but regasification capacity has not been specified.\n\nAL 2025: New mini LNG terminal (modular LNG terminal) proposed in the Bahamas (Nassau Cruise Port). Couldn't determine ownership structure of IPP; it might be owned by natural person(s) through Verdant and others, but it's unclear: \"The participants in EA Energy, and the renewable provider itself, were both described as having existing strong Bahamian links. EA Energy was said to have the Nassau Cruise Port as a “sister company”, although the link was not fully explained, while Verdant is a “shareholder in Island Power Producers\". The power plant supplied by the LNG terminal doesn't seem to be in GEM's database for now, but I added it for reference: \"The terminal, which will store 15,000 m3 of LNG, will provide natural gas to IPP's 60MW power plant, supplying shore power to cruise ships docked at Nassau Cruise Port.\"\nhttps://energy.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/oil-and-gas/inox-bags-contract-to-build-bahamas-first-mini-lng-terminal-for-cruise-ship-power/115168906. https://www.tribune242.com/news/2025/mar/31/new-power-plants-tap-investors-for-1325m/",
